Privacy enables individuals to make personal decisions without external interference, which is a cornerstone of autonomy.
Historical examples, such as the right to privacy in the US Constitution, show its role in safeguarding civil liberties.
Without privacy, individuals may face targeted discrimination or manipulation by entities with access to personal data.
Privacy fosters trust in relationships and institutions, which is vital for a functioning society.
Technological advancements have made surveillance more pervasive, making privacy a critical defense against abuse.